  "labels": {
    "anya": "Шеф",
    "anja": "Лёва",
    "angelina": "Федя"
  },
  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The angry chef screams at his lazy kitchen staff for leaving a dirty plate. He orders everyone to clean the kitchen immediately while they secretly plot to calm him down with a special spiked drink.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что за инвалиды?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой идиот тарелку здесь оставил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне что, за всем следить надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас есть полчаса, чтобы всё убрать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Совсем крыша у него поехала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Надо ему срочно дать выпить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да как? Тут же камеры кругом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, кто там ещё пришёл?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, хотите выпить чайку?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой к чёрту чай?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли вон, я вам сказал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, ну попробуйте этот чай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, вам обязательно понравится.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что за инвалиды?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a bunch of idiots?' Notice the word order: 'what' comes first, and 'disabled person' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой идиот тарелку здесь оставил?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Which idiot left a plate here?' Chef Viktor starts with 'idiot' and pairs it with 'plate' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне что, за всем следить надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do I have to keep an eye on everything?' Chef Viktor puts 'to me' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'what'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У вас есть полчаса, чтобы всё убрать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have half an hour to clean everything.' Listen for the word meaning 'you' right alongside 'there is' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Совсем крыша у него поехала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He has completely lost his mind.' Literally, 'his roof has completely slid off' — the classic Russian idiom for going crazy.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "angelina",
      "t": "Надо ему срочно дать выпить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to urgently give him a drink.' Fedya starts with 'it is necessary' and pairs it with 'him' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Да как? Тут же камеры кругом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But how? There are cameras all around here.' The first content word means 'how', and 'all around' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, кто там ещё пришёл?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, who else came there?' A compact expression built around 'who' and 'came'. Lyova answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, хотите выпить чайку?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, do you want to drink some tea?' The first content word means 'chef', and 'some tea' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой к чёрту чай?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What the hell kind of tea?' You'll hear 'the devil' all the time in the show, here connected with 'tea'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли вон, я вам сказал!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out, I told you!' In Russian, 'let's go' leads into 'said' in a very natural everyday phrasing. Then Lyova replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, ну попробуйте этот чай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, please try this tea.' Listen for the word meaning 'chef' right alongside 'try' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anja",
      "t": "Шеф, вам обязательно понравится.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, you will definitely like it.' Notice the word order: 'chef' comes first, and 'will please' finishes the sentence. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
